.TH UMEM_DEBUGGING 7 "2025-01" "libumem" "Miscellaneous Information"
.SH NAME
umem_debugging \- debugging guide for libumem on non-Solaris platforms
.SH DESCRIPTION
This manual page covers the runtime debugging features of
.B libumem
on Linux, FreeBSD, and macOS.  On Solaris these features were exposed
through
.BR mdb (1)
dcmds; on other platforms they are exposed through the
.BR umem (1)
command-line tool, the
.BR umem_inspect (3)
C API, and the GDB and LLDB integrations under
.IR tools/ .
.PP
The relevant debug modes are controlled by environment variables in
the target program, with no recompilation required.
.SH ENABLING DEBUG MODE
.SS Per-buffer audit (alloc + free stack capture)
.PP
.in +4
.nf
UMEM_DEBUG=audit ./myapp
.fi
.in -4
.PP
Records up to
.I umem_stack_depth
program counters into each buffer's bufctl audit record on every
alloc and free.  Required for
.B umem findleaks
and
.B umem whatis
to print stack traces.  Overhead approximately 30%.
.SS Transaction log (chronological history)
.PP
.in +4
.nf
UMEM_LOGGING=transaction=1m ./myapp
.fi
.in -4
.PP
Records every alloc and free event to a per-CPU ring buffer.  The
suffix accepts
.BR k ", " m ", or " g .
Required for
.BR "umem log" .
Overhead approximately 5%.
.SS Buffer guards (overrun and use-after-free detection)
.PP
.in +4
.nf
UMEM_DEBUG=guards ./myapp
.fi
.in -4
.PP
Adds redzone trailers and
.B 0xdeadbeef
fill on free.  Overrun, use-after-free, and double-free abort
immediately at the offending operation, calling
.B umem_event_error
so debuggers can break.  Overhead 10\(en20%.
.SS Combined debug
.PP
.in +4
.nf
UMEM_DEBUG=default ./myapp        \&# audit + contents + guards
UMEM_DEBUG=audit,guards ./myapp   \&# explicit combination
.fi
.in -4
.SH FINDING LEAKS
.SS Live process
.PP
.in +4
.nf
$ UMEM_DEBUG=audit UMEM_LOGGING=transaction=1m ./myapp &
$ umem --pid $! findleaks
.fi
.in -4
.PP
.B findleaks
reports buffers currently allocated, grouped by allocation stack
fingerprint and ranked by total bytes.  Output mirrors
.BR mdb (1)'s
.BR ::findleaks .
Buffers sitting in per-CPU magazines are subtracted (and reported as
.IR cached_skipped ),
so the count is the actual leak count.
.PP
For machine-readable output:
.PP
.in +4
.nf
$ umem --pid $! findleaks -f json | jq '.classes[0]'
.fi
.in -4
.SS Core dump
.PP
.in +4
.nf
$ umem --core core.12345 --exe ./myapp findleaks
$ umem --core core.12345 --exe ./myapp log -n 100
.fi
.in -4
.SS Offline (binary snapshot)
.PP
Take a snapshot in production:
.PP
.in +4
.nf
$ umem --pid $(pgrep myapp) snapshot /var/log/umem/$(date +%H).ums
.fi
.in -4
.PP
Analyze it later, anywhere:
.PP
.in +4
.nf
$ umem --dump /var/log/umem/14.ums findleaks
$ umem --dump /var/log/umem/14.ums status
$ umem --dump /var/log/umem/14.ums log
.fi
.in -4
.SH INTROSPECTION COMMANDS
The
.BR umem (1)
CLI, the
.BR gdb (1)
.B umem
prefix, and the
.BR lldb (1)
.B umem
prefix accept the same set of commands.  Inside a debugger:
.PP
.in +4
.nf
(gdb) source /path/to/tools/gdb/umem_gdb.py
(gdb) umem help
.fi
.in -4
.PP
.RS
.TP 14
.B findleaks
group allocated buffers by stack fingerprint
.TP
.B log
chronological transaction log
.TP
.B status
per-cache stats (\fB::umastat\fR equivalent)
.TP
.B walk
stream every entry (\fIallocated\fR, \fIfreed\fR, \fIlog\fR)
.TP
.B whatis
identify a pointer (\fB::whatis\fR equivalent)
.TP
.B bufctl
full audit record (\fB::bufctl_audit\fR equivalent)
.TP
.B snapshot
binary or text dump of allocator state
.TP
.B break
set conditional breakpoints on alloc/free/error events
.TP
.B events
arm or disarm the event hook subsystem
.RE
.PP
Each accepts
.B \-f text
(default) or
.B \-f json
output.  See
.BR umem (1)
for full syntax and
.BR umem_inspect (3)
for the C API.
.SH BREAKPOINTS ON MEMORY EVENTS
The functions
.BR umem_event_alloc ", " umem_event_free ", and " umem_event_error
are stable extern symbols the allocator calls at well-defined points.
With events enabled
.RB ( "umem events on"
or
.BR umem_inspect_enable_events(1) )
they are reached on every alloc, free, and detected corruption.
.PP
Set debugger breakpoints on them with arbitrary conditions:
.PP
.in +4
.nf
(gdb) umem events on
(gdb) umem break alloc -s 1048576    \&# break on >= 1 MB allocations
(gdb) umem break free  -a 0x7fa9b0c00000
(gdb) umem break error               \&# break on detected corruption
.fi
.in -4
.PP
This is the missing piece compared to mdb's \fB::stop\fR /
.B ::cont
workflow:  it lets you stop the program at the moment of interest
without recompiling or restarting.
.SH PERFORMANCE IMPACT
.TS
l l l .
Feature	CPU overhead	Memory overhead
_
guards	10\(en20%	~100 bytes / alloc
audit	~30%	~500 bytes / alloc
logging	~5%	log buffer size
default	50\(en70%	~600 bytes / alloc
.TE
.PP
.B UMEM_DEBUG=audit
disables PTC (per-thread cache) for affected size classes; allocator
takes the slow path through the magazine layer for those buffers.
This is necessary for correct audit-record bookkeeping.
.PP
Use the
.B UMC_NODEBUG
cache flag on
.BR umem_cache_create (3)
to opt specific hot caches out of debug instrumentation.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.SS Empty stack traces
On Linux x86_64,
.BR getpcstack (3)
falls back to
.BR backtrace (3),
which requires either frame pointers
.RB ( -fno-omit-frame-pointer )
or DWARF unwind info.  Distro-shipped binaries normally have one or
the other; aggressively-stripped builds may not.  Without unwind
info, recorded stacks are short or empty.
.SS findleaks reports more buffers than expected
Check the
.I cached_skipped
field: buffers currently parked in per-CPU magazines are reported
separately.  Their inclusion in the
.I total_buffers
count would be wrong; their exclusion is correct.
.SS findleaks reports allocator-internal caches
Use the C API
.B umem_walk_allocated()
directly to see them; the
.BR findleaks
filter skips
.B UMC_NOHASH
and
.B UMC_QCACHE
caches by default to keep the report focused on user allocations.
.SS Debugger expression evaluation aborts on fork
The default stack-trace formatter does not fork.  Source-line
resolution via
.BR addr2line (1)
is opt-in via
.BR UMEM_STACKTRACE_ADDR2LINE=1
because the fork interferes with debugger inferior calls.
.SH COMPARISON TO OTHER LEAK DETECTORS
.TS
l l l .
Tool	Live process	Production friendly
_
\fBumem\fR(1)	yes	yes (snapshot mode)
\fBvalgrind\fR(1) memcheck	full restart only	no (>10x slowdown)
AddressSanitizer	full restart only	no (>2x slowdown)
\fBjeprof\fR(1) (jemalloc)	yes	yes
.TE
.PP
.B umem
is the only of these that lets you attach to a long-running process
that was not started with special instrumentation
.RB ( UMEM_DEBUG=audit
counts as instrumentation, but has lower overhead than the others
and can be left on indefinitely).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR umem (1),
.BR umem_alloc (3),
.BR umem_cache_create (3),
.BR umem_debug (3),
.BR umem_inspect (3),
.BR gdb (1),
.BR lldb (1),
.BR mdb (1),
.BR valgrind (1)
.PP
.I tools/DEBUGGING.md
in the libumem source distribution covers full workflows and worked
examples.
.SH NOTES
.B UMEM_DEBUG
is intended for development, staging, and forensic post-mortem.
Setting it in production is acceptable for
.B audit
mode (~30% overhead, no behavioral change), less acceptable for
.B guards
or
.B default
modes.  The transaction log
.RB ( UMEM_LOGGING )
is cheap enough (~5%) to leave on permanently in many workloads.
.PP
Core dumps from processes running with
.B UMEM_DEBUG
enabled can be larger than usual due to per-buffer audit records and
log buffers; size on disk after compression is usually modest.
